  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is implemented and tested. The only part I could not check mechanically is the \"concrete case with expected behavior\" wording, which no tool can enforce.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `definition_blocks` in `src/lib.rs` is marked `{impl}` and parses the example fence.\n  - It accepts an opening fence of three or more colons followed by `{example} <name>`.\n  - It looks for a colon-only closing fence with `count >= fence_length`.\n  - It requires `valid_name`.\n  - It skips fences that overlap code blocks, so an example inside a fenced code block stays literal.\n- `collect_definitions` in `src/evidence.rs` is marked `{impl}` and does the rest.\n  - It builds the full name from `chapter_namespace` and `valid_name`, using the same path as `{req}`.\n  - The body is the text between the fences with option lines removed, then `.trim()`.\n  - It reports empty bodies, unclosed fences, and duplicates.\n  - Its final loop reports an error for any example that has no `{cite}` or `{test}` reference to a definition of kind Requirement. `reference_roles` skips escaped and protected (code) references.\n- `DefinitionKind` (the `Example` variant), `preprocess_book` and `replace_definitions` in `src/lib.rs` are marked `{impl}` and handle example definitions and their rendering. Nothing in the marked code adds behaviour the requirement does not specify; the extra `:test:` option handling is covered by `requirements.references.anchors`, which the code also marks.\n\n**Tests**\n- `examples_are_distinct_definitions_and_tests_link_to_them` (`src/lib.rs`) covers:\n  - a 4-colon outer fence containing a nested 3-colon example;\n  - the namespaced name `requirements.something.foo`;\n  - an invalid name (`bad name`), which stays literal;\n  - an example inside a code fence, which stays literal;\n  - a `{test}` link from another chapter.\n- `examples_may_reference_their_requirement_with_cite_or_test` (`src/evidence.rs`) covers:\n  - an example that uses `{test}`, which is accepted;\n  - an example that uses `{cite}`, which is accepted;\n  - an example that cites only another example, which is rejected.\n- `example_errors_include_duplicates_invalid_bodies_and_missing_tests` covers:\n  - a duplicate example name;\n  - an empty body;\n  - a missing closing fence;\n  - an example with no reference.\n- `cited_example_with_a_test_and_matching_evidence_validates` covers the positive end-to-end case.\n\nTogether these cover the interesting points in the design space.",
